WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.062
Django routes requests through URL
patterns into function-based or class-based

2
00:00:07.142 --> 00:00:07.712
views.

3
00:00:07.792 --> 00:00:13.879
Those views receive data from many places:
path parameters, query strings,

4
00:00:13.959 --> 00:00:19.189
form fields, JSON bodies, headers,
cookies, and uploaded files.

5
00:00:19.269 --> 00:00:22.027
Some inputs arrive from a browser,

6
00:00:22.107 --> 00:00:28.954
while others may come from mobile clients,
scripts, webhooks, or internal services.

7
00:00:29.034 --> 00:00:35.691
The secure default is to treat request
data as untrusted until it has been validated

8
00:00:35.771 --> 00:00:39.385
for the specific operation being
performed.

9
00:00:39.465 --> 00:00:45.932
Validation should check type, shape,
format, range, size, and business intent.

10
00:00:46.012 --> 00:00:52.384
A value can be syntactically correct and
still be inappropriate for the action.

11
00:00:52.464 --> 00:00:57.504
A user identifier can be an integer but
refer to another tenant.

12
00:00:57.584 --> 00:01:03.195
A file can have an expected extension but
contain unexpected content.

13
00:01:03.275 --> 00:01:09.266
A JSON body can have the right fields but
describe an action the caller is not

14
00:01:09.346 --> 00:01:10.677
allowed to take.

15
00:01:10.757 --> 00:01:14.847
Validation narrows what the application
accepts,

16
00:01:14.927 --> 00:01:21.108
while authorization decides whether the
current subject may use that data in

17
00:01:21.188 --> 00:01:21.949
context.

18
00:01:22.029 --> 00:01:28.401
Django forms and model validation concepts
can help make input rules explicit.

19
00:01:28.481 --> 00:01:34.187
Forms are especially useful when a request
maps to a known set of fields,

20
00:01:34.267 --> 00:01:38.356
constraints, and user-facing validation
errors.

21
00:01:38.436 --> 00:01:42.335
Model validation can protect data
consistency,

22
00:01:42.415 --> 00:01:47.931
but it does not automatically capture
every request-specific rule,

23
00:01:48.011 --> 00:01:52.100
authorization decision, or workflow
constraint.

24
00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:57.601
Developers should be clear about where
validation happens and why,

25
00:01:57.681 --> 00:02:03.292
rather than assuming that one layer covers
every path into the system.

26
00:02:03.372 --> 00:02:08.983
Safe request handling also includes method
discipline, size limits,

27
00:02:09.063 --> 00:02:11.630
and controlled error behavior.

28
00:02:11.710 --> 00:02:17.702
State-changing actions should use
appropriate methods and protections.

29
00:02:17.782 --> 00:02:24.534
Large bodies and uploads should have
reasonable limits before they consume memory,

30
00:02:24.614 --> 00:02:26.611
storage, or parser time.

31
00:02:26.691 --> 00:02:33.253
Error responses should help legitimate
clients fix bad input without disclosing

32
00:02:33.333 --> 00:02:39.229
stack traces, model internals, secrets,
raw queries, or internal paths.

33
00:02:39.309 --> 00:02:45.300
Well-structured views make security review
and maintenance much easier.
